---
description: Handling document upload and indexing, selecting documents from the knowledge base across modules, and best practices for file types
alwaysApply: false
---


- Central config.ts for document types
Use the single config file that defines, for each module, the accepted document formats. This file must be the only source of truth — for validation, user-facing error messages, and display of allowed formats.
Also, always use MAX_FILE_SIZE_MB and TOTAL_MAX_FILE_SIZE_MB (if multiple uploads are allowed) from the config.

- Cross-module uploads and indexing
If you upload a file into docs_autofills, you must also launch a Docs_Autofill index. Otherwise, it will appear as part of the Knowledge Base (and thus queryable), which can cause backend errors if it hasn’t actually been indexed.
If the following action requires the file to be fully processed (for example, if you need to query it), you cannot simply upload → index → trigger your task.
You must launch the workflow (see launch-workflows-after-indexing-documents-in-knowledge) so the action runs only once the files are ready.
For Smart Processing, no need for full readiness — it can run in parallel. For Smart Drafting, however, the file must be ready because it is queryable.
Note: the Upload button in the File Selector does not wait for file readiness before proceeding. Keep that in mind

- File Selector rules
The general File Selector must restrict selectable files to the formats allowed by the module, based on the central config.
Example: for Anonymization, if only certain doc types are allowed (e.g. .doc and .docx), the selector must restrict both direct uploads and Knowledge selections accordingly (this inconsistency was fixed).
Currently, only local files (type “file”) are supported. Supabase stores only a one-time expiring link from providers like Google Drive or OneDrive, so the original file cannot be retrieved later. (This is a fix I still need to handle.)
Now, when using FileSelector, you can add allowMultiple and allowedFileExtensions props.

- Copying documents between modules
Do not duplicate a document by simply copying a database row or reusing the filepath. The filepath does not include the bucket name.
Examples:
	•	A Docs_Autofill file belongs to the docs_autofills bucket.
	•	An Anonymization file belongs to the anonymization bucket.
If you copy only the filepath without moving the actual file, the frontend will search in the wrong bucket and fail to load it.
When duplicating or transferring documents between modules (especially involving Knowledge), physically copy the file from the source bucket (docs_autofills) to the destination bucket (e.g. anonymization) before creating the new record.

- Single vs multiple file uploads
Keep the behavior consistent between upload and selection.
If a module allows uploading only one file, the File Selector must also allow selecting only one file.
A new prop allowMultiple (default true) controls this — for example, Anonymization should set it to false.
Even if allowing multiple uploads (like in Knowledge) is more user-friendly, it’s not trivial. You must:
	1.	Upload each file individually to storage via the frontend.
	2.	Add the corresponding rows to Supabase (one by one or in batch).
	3.	When triggering the main action (e.g. Anonymization), make a single Edge Function call with the complete list of files — not one call per file.

Before doing this, make sure the backend supports multi-file processing. Otherwise, you’ll spawn one instance per file, and if a user uploads 100 files, it could crash the system.

- Fetching docs from Knowledge
If you fetch docs or folders from Knowledge (not via the File Selector), always use the helper functions from SupabaseUtils.tsx — namely fetchDocs and fetchFolders.
These functions automatically handle user permissions, so do not query docs_autofills manually.
You can specify filters like IDs, status lists, types, or accessibleByUserId.
For status:
	•	If the document must be processed (e.g. for a search feature), use statusList = ["ready"].
	•	If you only need the file itself, you could theoretically fetch also "processing" and "error" statuses (to be tested further).